me code review so it  could be in the middle depending on the  time zones that you have also  to serve right there could be rotations  a developer takes over a code review per  day or per week right there could be  behavioral mechanisms that you say well  whenever you're sending out a code  review you're asking for a code you you  do one in return and there are also  algorithms that can help you like round  rubbing or load balancing algorithms and  tools that help you to actually assign  it automatically but you have to be  aware that your your strategy here has  to reflect your goals right finding  defects improvements awareness mentoring  and teaching tracing and tracking right  if i want to find defects i definitely  have to have a person that's more  experienced and the best is a person  that has seen this code before we have  seen that in studies  if you want to have mentoring and  teaching maybe you want a junior  engineer here and now we're coming back  to the risk well then some automatic  approaches where you have like a round  robin are not the best because a high a  high change high risk change for example  shouldn't be assigned to a junior  engineer  so it's a little bit more complicated  here but what you can definitely do is  you can work on smaller reviews that's  so important right small and curious  changes and how you do that well with  feature flagging so you're working on  smaller things and you just push that  out  in production even right  while you're still developing it it also  means that your heavy branching and  using git to really make sure that  you're reviewing smaller code things  so this is the study also from cisco  where we see the correlation between  logs lines of code under review and time  and you see that there is a correlation  right it goes down and it's not the same  as before because here it doesn't matter  how long time how long do people have  time so you can give them two hours a  day  we still see that they're not finding  the defects if there are so many things  and we did a similar study at microsoft  and here you see not the lines of code  but the number of files in the change  set and you see it goes down right so  the percentage of useful comments  decreases drastically with the number of  files in the change set  so you want  independent and self-contained  code changes and this is a little bit  tricky but let's have a toy example  let's say you're working on a feature  and while you're in  the first file right you see some  something and you you know enact the boy  scout rule and you say well i'm renaming  this here it's a refactoring and then  you see a fix right you have to fix  something because you revealed it back  while doing this feature and so you're  actually going to other  files and you're doing things here  well now you're sending this out because  it's triple to you right it's called the  expert blind spot again right so it's  really trivial to you but it's actually  hard for the others to understand it  would be much better if this you know  would be extracted in one pull request  or in one code review and then a fix  would be in one code and the feature in  one because then you have coherent  a coherent file or change set and it's  more easy for the other person to  understand and understanding code  comprehension is really tricky in code  reviews right so you want actually to do  that and you could can use for example  this is just one approach there are many  many approaches but this is one approach  you could have like review branches for  that right so instead of committing  everything on one working progress pull  request and then have this  request  and people could also use like commit  based reviews uh but it's also nice if  you have like  each of those steps as a  separate branch which is on top of the  other branch which also means you can  send it out for review and can work  further and then you send it out for  review and work further and it's really  better for code comprehension to do it  that way  well this brings me somehow to the end i  know it was very short i was given 20  minutes so i tried to put as much  information as i could in there and i  really focused on the productivity part  here so really small coherent changes  are the key and there are a lot of  techniques that you can actually do to  do that um it takes a little bit of  training it takes a little bit of um  practicing a little bit of muscle memory  right a little bit like the single  responsibility principle also not comes  to you you have to actually learn it and  apply it  but then i think it's very very powerful  and you have to think about how to how  do we organize that that it's actually  fitting our goals right how are we going  to do code reviews as a collective as a  team so that nobody actually suffers you  know one has this pile of reviews and  the other does nothing um or you're  waiting too long  in general code reviews if you work on  your communication skills so important  knowing what to look for in code two is  really really important reducing review  burden is also very important also to  speed up code reviews  clear guidelines and policies very  important to speed them up and as i said  education and training if you have brown  bags around that really make code  reviews a topic that is discussed and  treated with respect  and deliberately  improved in your organization i think  this is very very important  so if you want  you can follow me on twitter and now i  think it's time for questions and the  discussion there is also a free ebook if  you want to download that and feel free  to reach out to me thank you so much for  listening to my talk
 

